    "summary": "Anthony Albanese and Opposition Leader Angus Taylor met twice in two days on the Interactive Gambling Amendment (Gambling Reform) Bill 2026. The Coalition wants inducement curbs aimed at VIP offers - exclusive events, game-day tickets, large bonus-bet packages - while leaving mass-market promotions around AUD25 untouched, plus a cooling-off period after a customer exits BetStop.",
    "why": "If the compromise lands where it is heading, the regulated object becomes the VIP inducement rather than the advertisement. That is a bonus-engine and CRM configuration problem: value thresholds, non-cash inducements and post-exclusion contact rules all have to become enforceable in the platform.",
    "expanded": "The two-day Senate hearing supplied the political fuel. Former NRL player Luke Bateman told senators a wagering VIP account manager offered him free interstate travel, race-day hospitality and allegedly illegal drugs, and told Senator David Pocock that senior company levels were 'not only aware but participating'. Sportsbet and TAB have denied the drug allegations. Greens spokesperson Sarah Hanson-Young said minister Anika Wells signalled willingness to amend on inducements but voiced no confidence in Taylor's negotiating strength. Labor wants Senate passage by the end of the parliamentary fortnight, with Albanese calling it 'the strongest anti-gambling legislation in Australia's history'. The Australia Institute puts Australian gambling losses at roughly AU$31.5bn in FY2022-23, the highest per capita in the world.",
