    "summary": "Optimove Insights, drawing on roughly 26 million monthly active sports bettors across the US, Europe and LATAM from April to July, found first-time depositor growth averaged 179% of baseline in the US, 194% in Europe and 314% in LATAM, while average bets per bettor fell to 92%, 75% and 65% of baseline respectively.",
    "why": "The two curves move in opposite directions, which means tournament cohorts cannot be evaluated on registration or FTD volume at all. Early-life value scoring has to run during the event, not in the post-mortem, or the acquisition spend is committed before the quality of the cohort is known.",
    "expanded": "During the final, first-time depositors rose from 139% to 257% of baseline in the US, from 217% to 254% in Europe and from 242% to 351% in LATAM - the sharpest single-match acquisition spike in the dataset. Average wager fell across every region: $266 to $236 in the US, $99 to $84 in Europe and $52 to $46 in LATAM. CEO Pini Yakuel framed the implication directly: 'More bettors does not automatically mean deeper engagement or higher long-term value... Operators now need to identify which customers are likely to become consistent, profitable players.' For platform teams the requirement set is specific - event-cohort tagging at registration, propensity scoring inside the first few sessions, and bonus mechanics that can differentiate between a one-match tourist and a convertible sports customer without a manual segment build.",
