    "section": "Industry Notes",
    "title": "Chris Grove: prediction markets will gamify trading rather than list casino contracts",
    "meta": "SBC Americas | 11 Aug 2026",
    "summary": "Eilers & Krejcik Gaming partner emeritus Chris Grove argues that people expecting literal blackjack or roulette event contracts are thinking about the threat the wrong way, and that the real direction is prediction markets making the act of trading itself more game-like.",
    "why": "The question of whether prediction markets will eat online casino has been framed around whether they can list casino games. Grove reframes it around trading mechanics, which is a much harder threat to legislate against and a much more plausible product path.",
    "expanded": "Grove's formulation: 'I don't think you'll see ‘casino contracts’ so much as you'll see prediction markets move more in the direction of offering trading overlays that are more engaging, entertaining, and higher volatility... put more simply, prediction market operators are likely to make trading more of a game.' He does not expect much cannibalisation of online casino revenue, on the basis that the two products serve different audiences, and cites an April EKG report finding 69% of event contract exchange volume comes from states that have not legalised sports betting. That figure is the load-bearing part of the argument - it implies the category is currently absorbing unmet demand rather than displacing licensed play. His view sits against warnings from Nevada Gaming Control Board Chairman Mike Dreitzer and Light & Wonder's Howard Glaser that casino-style contracts are a near-term threat, and against the AGA's estimate that states and tribes have lost over $1.2bn in tax revenue to prediction markets.",
    "commentary": "The disagreement between Grove and the Dreitzer/Glaser position is really a disagreement about what a casino product is. If it is defined by the game object, prediction markets are far away. If it is defined by session length, volatility and repeat cadence, then adding higher-volatility trading overlays gets to the same place without ever listing a slot.",
    "takeaway": "Watch the trading UI, not the contract list. The measurable signals would be shortening market durations, in-play style repeat trading and volatility-boosting overlays - all of which are product changes rather than new listings, and none of which would trigger the regulatory tripwires a casino contract would.",
    "source": "https://sbcamericas.com/2026/08/11/casino-style-prediction-markets/",
