    "title": "Paul Leyland: IG's Underdog deal is optionality across two products that may not both survive",
    "meta": "Earnings+More | 31 Jul 2026",
    "summary": "Regulus Partners' Paul Leyland reads IG Group's $1.3bn Underdog acquisition as a hedged bet across daily fantasy and prediction markets, calling the valuation 'essentially buy-one-get-one-free' optionality and questioning the choice of a DFS operator as the entry point.",
    "why": "Most coverage treated the deal as IG buying growth. Leyland's numbers undercut that reading directly and reframe it as IG buying two mutually hedging regulatory outcomes, which is a materially different investment case.",
    "expanded": "His central line: 'If DFS 2.0 comes under regulatory and competitive pressure but prediction markets thrive, or – less likely – vice versa, this will be a smart deal.' He notes it was 'almost impossible' for IG to ignore prediction markets given Robinhood's rapid expansion, but calls using a DFS operator as the entry point 'bold'. On valuation he benchmarks against Allwyn's PrizePicks purchase at roughly 7x EBITDA on a $2.5bn valuation versus IG paying around 9x annualised EBITDA for Underdog. The growth argument gets dismantled with one figure: IG itself grew revenue 18% in H1, roughly matching Underdog's growth rate, so the acquirer is not obviously buying a faster-growing asset. He also flags that DFS pick'em products face rising regulatory pressure now that prediction markets have undercut the 'only alternative to sportsbooks' defence DFS operators have relied on.",
    "commentary": "The hedging logic only works if the two regulatory outcomes are genuinely inversely correlated. There is a plausible scenario where both DFS pick'em and sports event contracts are squeezed together - state courts increasingly treat both as wagering by another name - in which case IG has bought correlated risk at 9x rather than optionality.",
    "takeaway": "Price the deal on regulatory correlation, not on growth. If a supplier is evaluating whether to build prediction market or DFS integrations, the same question applies: are these two demand streams or one, and does a single adverse legal doctrine take out both?",
