    "title": "Steve Ruddock: usage is rising and public opinion is falling at the same time",
    "meta": "Straight to the Point | 10 Aug 2026",
    "summary": "Steve Ruddock assembles polling from four sources to argue that American sports betting is in an unusual position - participation continues to grow while public sentiment deteriorates, which historically precedes restrictive legislation rather than expansion.",
    "why": "Operators tend to read participation growth as social licence. Ruddock's data separates the two and shows them diverging, which is the more useful leading indicator for where advertising, tax and product restrictions land next.",
    "expanded": "His thesis in one line: 'while there is increasing usage of mobile sports betting options, there is also increasing concern about the activity.' The July 2026 Siena poll found 39% of New Yorkers say online sports gambling is bad for the state, up two points year on year, while only 22% say it is good - down a striking 17 points - with 31% neutral. A Public First/Politico poll in May of roughly 2,000 respondents found 53% think sports event contracts should be legal against 23% who do not, notably higher support than for weather contracts at about 40% or election contracts at 27%. A Meredith College poll of more than 1,000 North Carolinians in July found broad acceptance of legal betting alongside demand for stronger safeguards, particularly around underage play and college athletes. An Urban Institute survey found 11% of US adults bet on sports in the past year and that while 65% reported no impact on personal finances, mobile bettors were 15 times more likely to miss a bill payment.",
